  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is an oscillating water column?",
      "a": "It is a hollow chamber, open below the waterline, in which waves make the trapped water rise and fall. The water acts like a piston, pumping air in and out through a turbine.",
      "asks": [
        "How does wave power work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is special about a Wells turbine?",
      "a": "Its blades are symmetrical, the same shape front and back, so air flowing either way pushes it round in the same direction. It keeps spinning as the chamber breathes in and out.",
      "asks": [
        "How does the turbine spin the same way both ways?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where does wave energy come from?",
      "a": "Wind blowing over the sea builds up waves and passes them energy, and the wind itself is driven by the Sun heating the Earth unevenly. So wave power is really solar energy, delivered by the sea.",
      "asks": [
        "How do waves get their energy?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is wave power not used more?",
      "a": "The sea is a harsh place: storms can smash machines, salt water corrodes metal, and waves come in every size. That makes wave power costly, so few machines have run for long.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is wave energy so hard?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where has wave power been tested?",
      "a": "The LIMPET oscillating water column ran on the Scottish island of Islay from 2000, and Mutriku in Spain has had one in its harbour wall since 2011. Orkney's European Marine Energy Centre tests many wave machines.",
      "asks": [
        "Are there real wave power stations?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The Mutriku wave power plant in Spain, built into a harbour breakwater, has fed the grid since 2011.",
    "The Wells turbine was invented by Alan Wells at Queen's University Belfast in the 1970s."
  ],
